<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Marshall Islands</h1><p class="st-meta">1986 CE – present · capital Majuro</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Pacific atoll republic in free association with the United States, once the site of 67 US nuclear tests, and a United Nations member since 1991.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Marshall Islands were part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> administered from 1947. On 1 May 1979 the United States recognised the constitution and the Government of the Republic of the Marshall Islands, with Amata Kabua as president. Full self-government came with the Compact of Free Association signed in 1986.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The 1979 constitution sets up a mixed parliamentary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/presidential-democracy">presidential system</a>, with the president chosen by the 33 senators of the Nitijela and a Council of Iroij of twelve paramount chiefs in an advisory role. Four of the first five presidents were traditional paramount chiefs. The trusteeship ended under Security Council Resolution 683 of 22 December 1990, and the islands joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> in 1991. In 1999 the United Democratic Party won the election and Kessai Note, a commoner, replaced Imata Kabua. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> provides defence and aid, and the US dollar is the currency.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>In January 2016 Hilda Heine became the first female president, and in January 2020 David Kabua, son of Amata Kabua, replaced her. Most financial aid under the Compact was set to expire in 2023, but was extended for another 20 years that year. The Nuclear Claims Tribunal assessed 2 billion dollars in compensation, which has never been paid, and concern continues over the Runit Dome.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/marshall-islands.jpg" alt="Marshall Islands" width="330" height="174"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Marshall_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1986 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Majuro</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Amata Kabua, Imata Kabua, Kessai Note, Hilda Heine, David Kabua</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christian (United Church of Christ - Congregational, Assemblies of God, Catholic Church)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Marshallese, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/marshall-islands">Marshall Islands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of the Marshall Islands</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=2006&amp;focus=marshall-islands" aria-label="Marshall Islands on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: where Marshall Islands lay"><image href="/maps/globes/g18000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4271 4395L2738 4107"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2738 4107h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4271 4395h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4271 4395h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Majuro. <a href="/atlas?year=2006&amp;focus=marshall-islands">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
